Without generics, you either lose type safety or repeat code:
// ❌ loses type info
function identity(value: any): any {
  return value;
}
const x = identity("hello");   // x is 'any' — no type safety

With generics

function identity<T>(value: T): T {
  return value;
}

const a = identity<string>("hello");   // T = string → returns string
const b = identity<number>(42);        // T = number → returns number
const c = identity("hi");               // T inferred as string ✅

<T> is a placeholder for a type. You (or TS) fill it in when calling — and the return type matches the input. Type-safe and reusable.

